WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.630
Film Courage: What does a good writing day
look like?

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:06.890
Mark Sanderson, Author/Screenwriter: A good
writing day looks like, I get up, I’ve had

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:13.519
a successful previous day writing and I’ve
left the script at a point where that I feel

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:19.679
that I’ve done my work yesterday so I’m
not feeling guilty or bad or like under pressure

00:00:19.679 --> 00:00:25.789
and I can just start up again and get my work
done in the morning, I mean you know email

00:00:25.789 --> 00:00:32.329
and all these other things and then just work
and take lunch and then come back and maybe

00:00:32.329 --> 00:00:34.079
5:00, 6:00 p.m. wrap out up.

00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:38.940
Now that depends on how many pages a day I
have to do and as I say, many days are good

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:45.940
solid days and other days are not so good
and not every scene is going to be on the

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:50.340
level and then there are other days where
it’s magical where you blow through a sequence

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:57.300
and you write two days in one day so you’re
ahead of the game, so it varies while you

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:06.720
are in it but it is kind of scary once that
starting gun goes off and you look at the

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.500
(I always look at the calendar because of
my due date and I’ve agreed) and then on

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:16.299
the last assignment we had an interaction
for some other reason about another project

00:01:16.299 --> 00:01:22.120
with them and I said “Oh by the way, I’m
target to turn it in on Monday” and they

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.130
said “Oh thanks,” you know?

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:24.130
And they didn’t bug me.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:27.790
The whole time they are not sniffing around
saying “Hey are you…”

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:29.100
You know?

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.100
Because they know.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.299
I’m not trying to pat myself on the back
but but they know because I’ve worked with

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:39.090
them before and they know they don’t have
to be sending that like “How’s it going?”

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.280
Email at being antsy.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.729
They just leave me alone which is a great
place to be.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:50.430
So a good writing day is like I said is having
a good…like I said if the day was crappy

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:54.800
before, you get up and you do it again.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:00.590
It’s day after day and it’s amazing what
you can accomplish in a month.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:04.600
I mean people think “Oh, this big ordeal
about writing a script you know, it’s going

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:05.960
to take me six months.”

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:09.440
It’s like maybe but if you structure your
time properly and I do have the time because

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:10.540
of my job.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.310
You structure you time properly, two pages
a day.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:19.760
You will have something finished and it’s
just doing it.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.660
And most people don’t do it, you know that’s
the thing.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:22.660
Film Courage: Why?

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.430
Why do you think they don’t do it?

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:27.460
Mark: I don’t know?

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:28.720
A lot of it is fear maybe?

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.750
A lot of it is “I’ll get to it tomorrow.”

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:35.020
There’s always something else to do and
it’s difficult and so if you’re writing

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:40.620
something that you don’t really care about,
why are you going to go back to it and just

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:46.480
be like Oh and if you are not getting paid
that another, I don’t want to say great

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.760
incentive but when you’re getting paid and
you sign a contract, that kicks your butt

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:52.790
into high gear.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:57.760
That’s what I’m saying when you write
in your specs you have to treat it as that.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.420
It’s like a second day job, it is.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:05.730
And I’m not saying you have to do 10 pages
a day but if you do two pages today and like

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:11.150
when I was working at the law firm for two
years and at lunch I would write, I would

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:15.570
write by hand but I wanted to not just sit
there and be like “Uh?”

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:20.260
So you know, everyday at lunch that was an
hour, that’s five hours a week, including

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.020
the time at night.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.170
It’s doable if you just plan it out.

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:33.340
Film Courage: So you’re looking at the due
date, then you’re looking at how many pages

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:39.290
and then you’re putting sort of a red X
on a calendar and you’re basically doing

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:40.790
how many pages a day?

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:44.460
Mark: Well, it depends on what I’m given.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.700
But this last job I worked and I was three
a day which was a luxury.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.560
Film Courage: But that’s not normal, right?

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.560
The three pages?

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:51.900
Mark: I mean three to five.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:56.660
The really hard scripts are eight to ten when
it’s a short period like a rewrite where

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:57.940
it’s like “We need this in two weeks.”

00:03:57.940 --> 00:03:59.370
Whoa!

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:03.230
You hang up the phone and you’re like “Ah…Okay!”

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:06.380
And like this last year, you’re always learning.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:10.770
I’ve had to become a faster writer, not
that I’m a slow writer.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:18.820
But I would take my time but not as fast as
and I knew if I was going to be taking more

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:22.010
jobs I would have to be a faster writer and
what?

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:24.220
Would I just do that on my own?

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:29.880
I was forced to do that by challenging myself
which I think is very important to always

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:34.200
challenge yourself and many times we don’t
challenge ourselves unless we come up against

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:40.340
something that challenges us and so that was
a particular job so I accepted the job and

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:41.820
I was like “Oh, wow.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:42.820
Okay?”

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.160
And I knew I could do it.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.900
But at first I didn’t think I could do it.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.910
And so I went back to my old techniques and
it’s funny sometimes I forget them and someone

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:56.490
says to me “You know you should buy this
book.”

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:57.840
And I go “really?”

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.480
And then I go “Oh, you mean my book?”

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.030
And they go “Yeah, you’ve forgotten your
disciplines.”

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.730
And I go “You’re right.”

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:11.100
I mean it’s like “Oh, yeah.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.560
You’re right.”

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.020
It’s funny.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:17.562
Film Courage: Incidentally (just to throw
this in there), your book [A SCREENWRITER’S

00:05:17.562 --> 00:05:18.562
JOURNEY TO SUCCESS: Tips, tricks and tactics
to survive as a working screenwriter in Hollywood]

00:05:18.562 --> 00:05:22.470
is now at the Santa Monica library which is
where you used to go as a teen?

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:27.030
Mark: Yes, in high school I used to work there
as a page which is shelving books.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:29.700
It was a nice clean job.

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:35.100
And so now how ironic that years later my
book is at the library that I used to work

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.860
at and I had to go in the other day (they
have four copies).

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:38.860
Film Courage: That’s pretty cool!

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:43.780
Mark: It is pretty cool and it’s right next
to…there’s John McKee and [John] Truby

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:47.010
and Screenwriting for Dummies and then there’s
my book and I’m like “Wow?”

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:53.250
And do a double take and it’s been checked
out which since they’ve got it, it’s been

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.030
out, people checking it out, so that’s good.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:56.820
Film Courage: And you came across that by
accident?

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:59.510
You just kind of went in there and…?

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:06.090
Mark: Yeah, they have some of my DVD’s of
movies that I’ve written and they order

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:13.900
things however and it was cool because it’s
nice that people can access it.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:19.300
It’s the kind of book that is not just a
read it one time, you can go back to it many

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:20.300
times I think.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:23.990
That’s how I wrote it where you can pick
it off the shelf and look at the particular

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:30.110
things I’m going through this problem and
maybe it would help you with that type of

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:34.480
thing, that’s how I wanted to write it was
the fact that it was something you could keep

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.050
next to your computer.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:40.630
And some books you read once and that’s
it.

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:45.560
It’s about all these things I’ve been
talking about, surviving in the trenches as

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.890
a writer or living a writer’s life.

